<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/18th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty · Peace</p><h1 class="enc-title">Treaty of Fort Stanwix (1784)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1784">1784</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-revolutionary-war">American Revolutionary War</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Treaty of Fort Stanwix of 1784 was signed on 22 October 1784 at Fort Stanwix, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a>, between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> and representatives of the Six Nations after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-revolution">American Revolution</a>. American commissioners, holding hostages, forced the Iroquois to give up claims to lands in western New York and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pennsylvania">Pennsylvania</a> and to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ohio">Ohio</a> Country. The confederacy council later rejected it, but it opened the way for large land cessions.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/treaty-of-fort-stanwix-1784.jpg" alt="Treaty of Fort Stanwix (1784)" width="320" height="190" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pennsylvania_land_purchases.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-paris-1783">Treaty of Paris</a> that ended the Revolutionary War, the British ceded their claims in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a> to the American government against the wishes of their Native American allies, and the status of Indian lands was ignored. Many Haudenosaunee fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a> to go on receiving British support; some later returned to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ohio">Ohio</a> region, where they often came into violent conflict with colonists trying to settle the area.</p><p>The treaty was meant both as a peace between the Americans and the Haudenosaunee and as a way to secure lands farther west, which the Haudenosaunee had gained by conquest during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beaver-wars">Beaver Wars</a> of the previous century. It was the first of several treaties between Native Americans and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> after the American victory.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-brant">Joseph Brant</a> led the Haudenosaunee at the start of the negotiations. He told the commissioners that his delegation had been sent to make peace and was not authorised to agree to any particular cession of lands. Brant had to leave early for a planned journey to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, and the leading Native representatives who signed were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cornplanter">Cornplanter</a> and Captain Aaron Hill. Under the treaty the confederacy gave up all claims to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ohio">Ohio</a> territory, a strip of land along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niagara-river">Niagara River</a> and all land west of the mouth of Buffalo Creek. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pennsylvania">Pennsylvania</a> the land gained is known as the Last Purchase.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The Six Nations council at Buffalo Creek refused to ratify the treaty, denying that its delegates had the power to give away such large tracts, asking for the return of the deeds and offering to repay any presents received. The wider Indian confederacy also disavowed it, since most of the Six Nations did not live in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ohio">Ohio</a> territory, and the Ohio Country peoples, among them the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shawnee">Shawnee</a>, the Seneca-Cayuga and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lenape">Lenape</a>, rejected it.</p><p>A run of treaties and land sales followed: Fort McIntosh in 1785, Fort Finney in 1786, the Phelps and Gorham Purchase in 1788, Fort Harmar in 1789, Canandaigua in 1794 and Big Tree in 1797. The war had already weakened the confederacy, and the negotiations at Fort Stanwix divided it further; it never regained its former influence.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Treaty of Fort Stanwix (1784)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
